# ~pgaupload/.ssh/authorized_keys on paxsor (ftp.pgadmin.org)
#
# The same two keys as on procyon, with the same options and for the same
# reasons; read the annotations in authorized_keys.procyon first, since they
# are not repeated here. What differs is the blast radius, because this host
# serves the packages that end users install, and the yum and apt signing keys
# live here.
#
# Placeholders in angle brackets must be substituted at install time:
#
#   <ROADIE-IPV4>, <ROADIE-IPV6>   the runner's addresses
#   <...KEY...>                    the public key, one line, no line breaks
#
# Note that these are the same two key pairs as on procyon, not four. One
# publishing key and one upload key, each trusted by both hosts, each confined
# differently on each host by the options below. Two more key pairs would mean
# two more things to rotate and no reduction in what a compromised runner can
# reach, since a compromised runner has both of them anyway.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 1: publishing. Verbs only.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What a holder of this key can do on paxsor: create a v<VERSION> or snapshot
# directory (refusing if it already exists), pull the matching staging content
# from procyon, rebuild and sign the apt and yum metadata for an allowlisted
# distribution, and run the four existing publication scripts with arguments
# that have been validated or allowlisted first.
#
# What it cannot do, and this is the point of the exercise: delete anything,
# name a file, name a path, name a host, name a signing key, choose which
# binary runs, or supply a single character that reaches a shell.
restrict,from="<ROADIE-IPV4>,<ROADIE-IPV6>",command="/usr/local/sbin/pga-publish" ssh-ed25519 <...PUBLISH-KEY...> pga-publish@roadie

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Key 2: upload. Bytes only, and into a landing area rather than the live tree.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On procyon the upload key writes into the staging root, because staging is
# what it is for. Here it writes into a separate landing directory that nothing
# serves, so a compromised runner cannot put a file where a user's apt or dnf
# would find it: only the wrapper's verbs move content into the published tree,
# and only from a validated staging directory.
#
# In the normal release flow this key is not used at all, since publication
# pulls from procyon. It is here for the occasional out-of-band artefact that
# has no staging build behind it, and if review concludes that case does not
# exist, the right answer is to delete this line rather than to keep it warm.
#
# SITE: create /var/ftp/incoming owned by pgaupload, outside the document root
# and outside /var/ftp/pgadmin4, and confirm that nothing serves it.
restrict,from="<ROADIE-IPV4>,<ROADIE-IPV6>",command="/usr/bin/rrsync -wo -no-del -munge /var/ftp/incoming" ssh-ed25519 <...UPLOAD-KEY...> pga-upload@roadie
